*ChinAI #264
<https://substack.com/redirect/12c50cad-84c7-40bf-8ff9-968702b89087?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*).
This week, Sihang Song, a regular Huxiu contributor, highlights another
Chinese AI start-up that belongs in that first-tier: MiniMax. In the wake
of SoftBank selling its Nvidia stock and announcing that it would increase
its investment in OpenAI, Song begins with a provocative question: Which
Chinese foundation model player will come and challenge OpenAI?

*Key Takeaways: This essay=E2=80=99s take on the U.S. =E2=80=9CAI Bubble=E2=
=80=9D sees OpenAI and
Anthropic in an unsustainable position =E2=80=94 they must continuously bur=
n
through talent, capital, and computing power to maintain their position.*

   -

   Let=E2=80=99s lead off with capital. Song cites a Jefferies report that =
the
   total AI capital expenditure spending of leading Chinese cloud vendors o=
ver
   the past three years is 82%(!!!) lower than their US counterparts.

<https://substack.com/redirect/36d23122-204d-4cfc-ac85-f027dca6cc3b?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Jefferies
Equity Research report, November 2025

   -

   Next, the capex gap reflects a difference in compute usage too. For
   example, according to MiniMax=E2=80=99s technical report announcing thei=
r M1
   system, the model was trained for three weeks using 512 H800 chips at a
   total cost of ~$540,000. Song argues, =E2=80=9C*This proves a crucial po=
int:
   competition among large models isn=E2=80=99t necessarily about =E2=80=98=
who is bigger,=E2=80=99 but
   can also be about =E2=80=98who uses computing power more intelligently.=
=E2=80=99*=E2=80=9D

*What does Song see as MiniMax=E2=80=99s comparative advantage versus OpenA=
I? *

   -

   In many ways, MiniMax is following OpenAI=E2=80=99s model. Their revenue=
 model
   is also based on subscription revenues and API calls. From the Huxiu ess=
ay,
   =E2=80=9C*MiniMax=E2=80=99s ARR (annual recurring revenue) has reached $=
100 million this
   year. Huxiu has noted from multiple sources that a leading video platfor=
m,
   a large edtech company, and a global voice communication service provide=
r
   are all collaborating with MiniMax through APIs.*=E2=80=9D
   -

   The differences come from the AGI roadmaps. Song sees OpenAI as building
   a massive system that aims toward =E2=80=9Clong-term OS-ification=E2=80=
=9D, which is
   =E2=80=9Ccumbersome and requires a long period of capital and computing =
power
   support.=E2=80=9D In contrast, MiniMax is making immediately commerciali=
zed
   products rather than future platforms. To me, this is where Song=E2=80=
=99s logic
   crumbles a bit: =E2=80=9C*OpenAI is defining a longer-term AGI, while Mi=
niMax is
   simultaneously achieving AGI and seizing the crucial three-year window o=
f
   opportunity for future commercialization*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   Just my two cents: Any company that is just optimizing for a three-year
   window is completely misunderstanding the prolonged impact timeframe of
   when a general-purpose technology like AI will make its mark. To be clea=
r,
   I don=E2=80=99t think that=E2=80=99s what MiniMax is doing. I just think=
 Song=E2=80=99s analysis is
   flawed and reflects the huge holes in this grand narrative of U.S. AI
   optimizing for AGI while China=E2=80=99s AI is optimizing commercializat=
ion.

